### 3. Infrastructure Issues & Analysis
- **Why terminating SSL at the load balancer level is an issue:** 
  If SSL is terminated at the load balancer, traffic between the load balancer and the backend web servers travels in plain text (unencrypted). If an attacker breaches the internal network, they can sniff this internal traffic. It also places the heavy computational load of decryption/encryption entirely on the load balancer.
- **Why having only one MySQL server capable of accepting writes is an issue:** 
  Having a single write-capable server (Primary/Master) creates a bottleneck and a Single Point of Failure (SPOF) for write operations. If the primary database fails, the application cannot process new records, sign-ups, or data updates until a failover occurs.
- **Why having servers with all the same components (database, web server and application server) might be a problem:** 
  Coupling all components (web server, app server, and database) on the same machine creates resource contention. The database and application server compete for CPU, RAM, and disk I/O, making it harder to scale individual layers independently, optimize performance, or isolate failures.